Date: Wed, 8 Dec 1993 10:45:01 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Conceding Opponent's Points

Folks,

A half dozen or so of you have posted reactions, negative and postive,
to this snippet from my post a few days ago on Amara's thoughts:

> >>Never admit to your apponent in a debate that he is right and you are 
> wrong....


This has been interpreted as me saying I will never admit I am wrong,
that I will continue a pointless argument ad infinitum, etc. Not so.

In the context of discussing how males operate in the workplace (or
similar contexts, such as this list) differently from females, I was
offering an observation which is almost certainly correct: many men
will not "back down" in the midst of a discussion or argument, though
in the fullness of time they may accept the arguments. In fact, they
will "internalize" the former opponent's arguments so much so that
they can argue with newcomers, say, the same points they were once
arguing _against_. (For a concrete example of this, think of the folks
who were once opposing PPLs and private justice systems now explaining
them to skeptical newcomers.)

The classic example of this, which I've seen many times in my life and
even a couple of times on this list, is the conversion to libertarian
and anarchocapitalist views. To wit:

-- a person will be very skeptical of libertarian views, will argue
about the need for social justice, taxation of the exploiters, the
need to control monopolies, etc.

-- the libertarians will argue back about choice, voluntary trade, the
dangers of command economies, efficiency, rights, etc.

-- little conversion or concession of defeat happens during the argument.

-- the other person will seldom say: "You'r right. You have proved to
me that socialism is flawed. I am now a libertarian."

-- rather, what often happens is that the person (the one slowly
converting to libertarianism) will jump in on _other_ arguments,
perhaps with folks even newer to the list or the ideas of liberty, and
will begin making libertarian arguments!

-- essentially, two things, both positive, are going on here. First, a
Socratic method of talking to discover the truth, with the act of
_teaching_ others an essential part of the learning, Second, a
"dominance" situation in which the "younger monkey" teaches still
younger monkeys how to "do it." (Imagine a little boy who's just
learned how to do something saying to a still-younger boy: "Here, this
is how it works." This is common in little kids, in teenagers, and in
adults.  Contrary to the current view that little boys are sociopaths
and psychopaths, little boys are actually quite helpful to other
little boys.  Even on this list we have seen relatvie newcomers
"teach" the skills (opinions?) they have learned to even newer
newcomers. Over on the Cypherpunks list, this is becoming the modus
operandi, with each new generation of list members being taught by the
generation which arrived just before them.)

Thus, converts to positions are rarely made _in the debate_, but are
made afterwards, through actions. Time is apparently needed to
integrate the arguments, to resolve the conflicts, to internalize the
new information. Pure logic does not win debates. (I believe Tannen
made essentially the same point by noting that men will rarely
_concede_ an argument about some course of action during the debate,
but will effectively concede by assent, by not later protesting the
course of action. One ex-girlfriend of mine used to constantly want
"closure" on all arguments, badgering me with constant "Do you see
that you were wrong?" Sometimes I would eventually, after enough
badgering, cry "Uncle!" Mostly I would just shrug and say "Whatever,"
which was my way of saying: "I'm tired of this conversation. Maybe
you're right, maybe you're wrong, but I'm not going to resolve it
right now."  Needless to say, I was and am very far from perfect, but
my view of such debates, as expressed here, at least never caused me
to seek admissions of "wrongness" from her.)

This conversational/debating style is actually _less_ confrontational,
ironically, than many others. I don't expect to _ever_ convert a
person to, say, my "crypto-anarchist" position through one or even
several debates. Rather, I hope to argue my points in a series of
posts, a series of questions and answers, and then have _them_ begin
to make the same points themselves! This, to me, is satisfying.

My point about tipping off Amara to this style of debate was that many
women apparently don't realize this is common. It's not about "men
can't admit they are wrong," it's about _how_ they admit they once
_were_ wrong (emphasis on past tense is intentional), but have now
seen the light.

Having a "conversion experience" is a way acceptable to most males to
radically switch opinions, a kind of phase change. Perry Metzger, for
example, talks gleefully about his days as an ardent socialist and his
eventual conversion to libertarians outlooks, which he has been on a
stable basis for many years now.

(There are perhaps some resonances with the way pack animals behave.
The traits of the leaders are emulated by the junior or less-dominant
members of the pack. On this list, for example, the founders and ExI
members are all ardent libertarians, even radical anarchocapitalists,
so this establishes a kind of target for new members to gradually aim
for, or to take pots shots at, of course.)

So, the issue is not about blindly pursuing an argument on something,
but about the ways in which views change and are acknowledged.

I hope this clarifies things.

--Tim May


