First, the "Polly Klaas Bill" would not necessarily be linked to a
surge in executions, but to ending the revolving-door justice system.
In Calfornia, for example, the Klaas kidnapper and killer, was out on
parole after a long string of crimes (that I described earlier), while
20-year-old kids are facing another 20 years of prison under
"mandatory minimums wit no parole" for posssession of some acid at a
Grateful Dead concert.

I, too, am nervous about the death penalty, and would save it for
"red-handed" (blood on the hands, you know) cases. Direct witnesses,
blood on the hands, confessions, etc.

> Note that the average cost of a state execution in the US was put at
> about a million dollars by one program that I saw; the cost of keeping
> a prisoner in jail is about $50,000 a year. So it's not, in principle,
> much more expensive to run a British-style "serious" life imprisonment
> system than to execute felons. 

Let me tell you, a bullet doesn't any million dollars. That widely
cited figure is the result of a legal system that delays carrying out
the execution for 5 to 10 years of nearly constant appeals, all of
which are paid for by the taxpayers. Need I say more?

> Now seriously: I'm startled to see extropians discussing something
> that I consider to be the ultimate statist atrocity -- giving the bastards
> the _automatic_, _mandatory_ authority to execute people. Doesn't this
> strike any of you as incongruous? Here we are talking about boundless
> optimism and maximizing personal potential, and then suddenly the
> debate's moved onto bleating excuses to give the state more powers of
> life or death over us!
> 
> I'd like to see an end to this thread as fast as possible, or at least
> a turn in a more extropian direction.  How about discussing this:
> what kind of effective criminal sanctions would be appropriate to a
> PPL system when the damage caused by the assailant is greater than
> their total lifetime earning potential plus their insurance cover?

Good points, well worth discussing.

Charlie's mention of PPLs here is the key. When I say I am for the
death penalty in certain cases, I mean that I, for example, would
cheer if Polly Klaas's father took it upon himself to kill the
bastard.

I agree that having the State carry out sentance is not ideal. I don't
have a simple solution. I expect an Icelandic justice system, though
flawed, would not have a character like Davis wandering the
neighborhoods. But dealing with such career criminals needs more work.
(When I get some time, soon, I'll go back and read Benson and see if
"The Enterprise of Law" has anything persuasive to say about capital
crimes and capital punishment.)

(Heinlein's story "Coventry" may also have something of relevance.)

I note, however, that having the State incarcerate a person like Davis
for several years and then release him back in to the community, a
"rehabilitated man," is absurd.

If anyone deserved a "Poor Impulse Control" tattoo on his forehead, it
was he.

