I like Elias' description of default behaviors as a complex surface,
but I think that it should be viewed as having at least three levels
of generality. One is for the individual, and even that will change
over time. Next is for cultures (remember the stuff about which
sex coos over babies being culture-specific?), and the third is
for the whole species. If you're mostly interacting with people
from one culture, it's easy to mistake the culture surface for
the species surface.

Now that I think about it, they should be probability blurs that
are mostly surface-shaped rather than surfaces. An exception to
surfaceness would be that cultures don't just have typical "good
citizens", they also have typical deviants who'd form their own
surface.

