From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Vinge, The Singularity, and Despair

Re: Jupiter Brain Count

While 'a brain the size of Jupiter' began as a descriptive phrase,
Ms. Lebovitz asks an interesting question.  I will approach an
answer by talking about another Jupiter-sized object, a 'Bubble
World'.  Several years ago, at one of the Princeton Space Manufacturing
conferences, over dinner, we got into a discussion about what was
the largest possible space colony.  I went home and came up with
one answer that was a heck of a lot larger than the O'Neill colonies
that had been proposed before.  I got my result by jettisoning the
requirement to produce artificial gravity by spinning the whole
space colony.  Instead, I assumed that wherever you wanted
some artificial gravity, you would set up a local centrifuge, but
the space colony as a whole would not rotate.


To cut to the answer, you have a sphere of hydrogen gas about
3 times the diameter of Jupiter.  It has a density of 2 atmospheres at
room temperature at the center, and 1 atmosphere at the edge.
The pressure difference is from the self-gravitation of the
hydrogen.
Surrounding the hydrogen is a 2400 km thick layer of living space,
with air, people, houses, etc.  The air pressure varies from 1 atmosphere
at the bottom to .7 atmosphere at the top of the living space.  On top
of the living space is 500 meters of steel, which hold in the air by its weight.

The 'bubble world' is in pressure equilibrium throughout, so there
is no need for any pressure vessels with strength to hold something in.

You have 1400 times the surface area of the Earth, and by assuming
'decks' 2400 meters apart, over a million times the useable living
volume.  You have lots of surface area to dump waste heat over.  Allowing
a couple of hundred watts per brain-equivalent, you can support
724,000 trillion brain power.  The total mass of such a bubble world is
3 earths, mostly in the steel shell, so you can make maybe a dozen
out of the planet cores we have around.  If you mine the Sun for metals,
you might get a couple of hundred more.

If you try to get a bubble world bigger,
the self gravity of the hydogen core makes it smaller (keeping a
Earth-habitat skin).  If you instead want a large suface bubble
holding up a space-hardware skin (solar cells, computer chips,
radiators), you could make it a lot bigger, although I have not figured out
how large.

You might have a dust belt of 'brain modules' of nanoscale parts,
or an 'asteroid belt' of brain chunks of kilometer scale parts,
or a few planet-brains, or a mixture of all three, or a single
solar-brain, of all the above linked together by laser optics.
It might even look like a natural solar system for aesthetic reasons.

People think Jupiter gives off twice the heat as it receives light from the
sun because it is still cooling from its formation.  Maybe we're
wrong.  Maybe its just thinking.

Dani Eder

