From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Will cryptoanarchy keep you safe?

Nancy Lebovitz:

> I can't see that cryptoanarchy will do you much good if a government
> has decided that everyone in your region should die, or if there's
> a war where you're living. 

Protection against indiscriminate warfare and terrorism is still
primarily a physical matter.  Two possibilities:

* Live out in the country away from any important targets
* Live in the middle of a rich multi-ethnic region, where
it would be hard for a terrorist to strike without killing
their own kinds. (But again, there should be few important
targets, eg don't live in Manhattan).

Governments splattering blood all over the international
TV channels lose popular support. The careful media control
surrounding Tianenmen (massacre occured during the night with the
worst blood out of sight of TV cameras) and Waco (very strict 
control of press to beyond range where telephoto lenses could
see detail) can't last into the era of pocket telephoto camcorders
and decentralized news distribution.  As power goes from territories 
to virtual nations, such governments will evolve to resemble the 
undemocratic mafias of today.  

Worldwide, governments try to stifle huge amounts of trade
demanded by market participants.  "Markets want to be free", to mutate
a phrase, and so the trade in "black" markets goes on, even under 
constant threat of violence.  International mafias are already estimated 
to reap $1 trillion in profits worldwide, an amount nearly as great as 
the entire U.S. federal budget.  

If mafias are prototypes of anarcho-capitalist PPL's we're in big 
trouble, as they tend to bring violence into contract disuptes far 
sooner than most jurisdictions.  The slain Escobar especially is portrayed 
as a kind of underworld neanderthal, assasinating dozens of competitors 
and debtors with overdue bills, and giving himself away by making 
traceable telephone phone calls to his family.  (Teenager Escobar Jr. 
proved smarter than his dad, warning him as soon as he called to get 
off the phone, but it was too late).  I hope that the new generation 
of mafias will be based much more on rational arbitration of contract 
disputes and much less on violence, but the a nonviolent mafia culture 
may take a long time to evolve.  The evolution of mafias 
may be as important as the evoltuion of governments in 
bringing us to the anarcho-capitalist world we strive for.

An excellent article on mafias in this week's Newsweek, if you can 
ignore the usual amount of detweiling the press indulges in when 
talking about these issues.  (For example, complete confusion of
nonviolent business activities of the mafias with the violent
means they often resort to when forced to operate not only
outside, but antagonistic to, jurisdictions).

